ween my teammate and the Landlord, playing a card now (like K, A, or 2) would involve beating my teammate\'s card, which is generally counter-productive unless I can guarantee a win or significantly disrupt the Landlord. The Q is high enough to force the Landlord to spend a King, Ace, 2, or Joker if they wish to play. I hold three 2s, which are critical control cards. It is best to save them to beat the Landlord\'s cards directly rather than spending one now to beat my teammate.\n\nReason: Helping the teammate by not interfering with their high card (Q) allows the pressure to pass to the Landlord. The Landlord, with 7 cards remaining, must decide whether to use a top card or pass. Preserving my high cards (Aces and 2s) is crucial for blocking the Landlord\'s future attempts to run.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
